  <mxGeometry x="760" y="10" width="600" height="20" as="geometry"/>
</mxCell>
<mxCell id="lg_color_t" value="Card header colour: blue core, green leaf, red firewall. Solid box: data center; dashed box: CloudFabric fabric. Data center 2 mirrors Data center 1; the link subnets (172.16.100.0/24 in DC1, 172.16.200.0/24 in DC2) and the VNI ranges (5000-9999 in DC1, 10000-15000 in DC2) differ." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=10;fontColor=#000000;" vertex="1" parent="legend">
  <mxGeometry x="16" y="34" width="1568" height="20" as="geometry"/>
</mxCell>
<mxCell id="lg_note_t" value="The firewall and the core use only the public routing table; each peers eBGP with the EXT VRF, a VPC on the border-leaf pair (the FW with each member&#x27;s M-LAG IP .3/.4, never the shared .2); no PBR. Each VPC VRF has a default route to its FW sub-interface; the FW reaches local VPC segments by static route and the other DC&#x27;s hosts by the /32s EXT sends it, advertises nothing, and sends north-south traffic to EXT by a static default route. EXT imports the local hosts&#x27; /32s by logical switch RT and keeps them out of its routing table (rib-only); the server leaves tag them with the DC&#x27;s community, so EXT sends the FW only the other DC&#x27;s. The cores carry Layer 3 between the DCs and reach the local VPC subnets by a static 10.0.0.0/8 to EXT. VPC subnets are stretched by the transit switch on the DCI leaves, which joins each DC&#x27;s own VNIs through a transit VNI from the MDC&#x27;s global range (MC-01)." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=10;fontColor=#000000;" vertex="1" parent="legend">
  <mxGeometry x="16" y="58" width="1568" height="34" as="geometry"/>
</mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
